EXAMPLE_TEXT = """The Little Network That Learned to Write

Once there was a little network that lived inside a laptop. It had no eyes and no hands, only numbers, and every night it dreamed of letters. The laptop was small and warm, and the little network liked to sit in the quiet between the fan and the screen and listen to the keys.

One day a person opened the laptop and gave the network a long text. The text was full of words: words about rivers and roads, about bread and snow, about friends who walk home together when the lamps are lit. The network did not understand a single word. It saw only a line of characters, one after another, like beads on a long string.

"Read it," said the person. "Read it, and try to guess the next letter."

So the network began. It looked at the first letters and guessed the next one. It was wrong. It guessed again, and it was wrong again. Each time it was wrong, it changed its numbers a little, the way a river changes its bed a little with every spring. The person watched the loss go down, slowly, like the sun going down behind the hills.

After a while the network noticed things. After a dot there was often a space, and after the space a capital letter. After the letter q there was almost always the letter u. The word the came again and again, and so did and, and so did little. When a line began with a quote, the quote came back a few words later. The network did not know what these things meant, but it knew that they were true.

The person said, "Now let me show you what you have not seen." And the person took some pages away and kept them hidden. The network had never read those pages. It tried to guess their letters too, and sometimes it was right. The person smiled. "That is the real test," the person said. "Anyone can repeat what they have read. To guess well on a page you have never seen, you must have learned something."

The network trained for a long time. It learned that rivers flow and roads wind, that bread is warm and snow is cold, that friends walk home together when the lamps are lit. It learned that a sentence has a beginning and an end, and that the end usually comes with a dot. It learned that some words like to stand next to each other, and that other words never meet.

Then the person typed a few words and said, "Go on." The network looked at the words and guessed the next letter, and then the next, and then the next. At first it wrote strange things, words that were almost words, like a child who sings a song before knowing it. But the more it learned, the more its words looked like the words in the long text. It wrote about a river that runs past a little house. It wrote about a road that goes over the hill and down to the sea. It wrote about two friends who walk home together when the lamps are lit.

"You are writing," said the person.

"I am guessing," said the network, if a network could speak. "I am only guessing the next letter, one at a time."

"That is all writing is, at the beginning," said the person. "One letter, then the next. One word, then the next. You learned it from the text, and the text came from people, and now a little of it lives in you."

The little network was very happy. It did not know many words, and it did not know the world. But it knew the shape of the text it had read, the way a river knows the shape of its valley. And every night, when the laptop was closed and the fan was quiet, it dreamed of letters, one after another, like beads on a long string, and it guessed which one would come next.

In the morning the person came back with a new text. This one was about the sea: about boats and nets, about the wind that comes before the rain, about a lighthouse that stands alone on a rock and turns its light all night. The network read it, letter by letter, and guessed, and was wrong, and changed its numbers a little, and guessed again. The loss went down. The hidden pages stayed hidden. And slowly, the little network learned to write about the sea.

The end.
"""
